2026-11-14T00:00:18Z (RFD66606-2494) 'The sample is its own repository in our organisation, started on 2026-11-14.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2494  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-14T00:00:19Z (RFD66606-2494) '- The Mojo compiler is pinned to a 1.3.0 nightly. RISC-V emission was missing from nightlies after 1.0.0b3.dev2026071006 and through at least 1.1.0.dev2026081505, and works in the pin.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2494  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-14T00:00:20Z (RFD66606-2494) '- It ran in the engine's double-precision Sandbox build in a real window, and in the safe-Rust libriscv port, which has no Sandbox host. A Godot build without the Sandbox class fails it.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2494  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-14T00:00:21Z (RFD66606-2494) 'The limits
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2494  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-14T00:00:22Z (RFD66606-2494) 'The tests are in its tests directory. They run in the Rust port, and the string test also runs in the engine, because a string lives in the host. Every property holds today: int, bool, float, string and object Variants, one worker, parallelize on one worker, and a spin lock under tasks. The Wait_Timeout test passes in the engine, where the guest reads Godot's clock, and is skipped in the Rust port, which has no host clock. A vmcall of an echo function returns each of the five types unchanged in the engine. A host that passes native arguments gets its own function, add_numbers_native, which returns 7 for 3 and 4. Only that function has a native form. Nothing is red. A timed wait polls the clock and each poll is charged to the instruction budget, so the engine test sets the budget to no limit.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2494  1 LINE
 Text:Written
